[Unit]
Description=HDW4S headless desktop for %i
Documentation=man:hdw4s(8)
After=network-online.target hdw4s-firewall.service
Wants=network-online.target
# Deliberately a hard requirement. Stopping the firewall unit runs its
# ExecStop=, which deletes the table that is the only thing standing
# between this session and the network, so a session must not outlive it.
# The package is careful never to stop it during an upgrade.
Requires=hdw4s-firewall.service

# WHICH INCARNATION OF THIS DESKTOP IS RUNNING, published where the occupant
# cannot forge it and any page this session serves can read it -- exactly as the
# ephemeral unit does, and for a reason a named desktop turns out to share.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-25, by the owner on his own named desktop: a tab that is
# hidden and comes back meets the reconnect card EVERY TIME. Its console shows
# `/hdw4s-incarnation 404` on the first line. The gate's resume path compares
# the identity this tab connected to against the one published in the web root,
# and a MISSING file is a refusal -- failing closed, which is right, into a card
# shown on every single return with nothing behind it.
#
# So the repair that fixed this for the pool could not work here, and nothing
# said so: every rig ran against ephemeral slots, and the one thing a named
# desktop has that an ephemeral one does not is the ABSENCE of this file.
#
# Wants=, deliberately, not Requires=, for the reason the ephemeral unit states
# at length: the postcondition in "hdw4s-webroot check" already refuses to start
# a session whose token is missing, unchanged, or not the one this generation
# wrote, which catches more cases than Requires= would -- and Requires= would
# charge a stop-propagation footgun for it, where stopping a dead oneshot takes
# a live desktop down with it. After= is what actually orders it.
Wants=hdw4s-incarnation@%i.service
After=hdw4s-incarnation@%i.service
# A home directory that arrives over the network has to be there before a
# desktop tries to read settings out of it.

[Service]
# There is deliberately no User= here. The instance name is the account name,
# so "User=%i" would be correct as far as it goes -- but an invalid user name in
# a base unit is a fatal parse error, and the unit then never loads far enough
# for a drop-in to override it. An instance named for an account that does not
# exist would become unloadable rather than failing with something an
# administrator can read. So "hdw4s enable" writes User= into a drop-in for
# every instance, and hdw4s-session refuses to run as root, which is what a
# missing drop-in would otherwise mean.

# A narrow bounding set, and three capabilities in it.
#
# CAP_NET_RAW is here because an empty set breaks ping in a way that takes a
# long time to understand: the distribution ships /usr/bin/ping with
# cap_net_raw=ep, and the kernel refuses to execve a binary whose effective
# capabilities cannot be granted, so the session gets "Operation not permitted"
# from exec with nothing said about sockets. Allowing a capability into the
# bounding set does not hand it to anything: a process still needs it on its own
# file to use it, and setting that needs CAP_SETFCAP, which is not here. Packet
# capture stays impossible regardless, because AF_PACKET is not in
# RestrictAddressFamilies below.
#
# CAP_SETUID and CAP_SETGID are here because leaving them out does not do what
# it looks like it does. Without them a setuid-root program cannot change its
# gid, so sudo fails at "unable to change to root gid: Operation not permitted"
# -- before sudoers is consulted and before anyone is asked for a password. It
# reads as a security control and is not one. Measured, on a setuid-root binary
# run with exactly the bounding set that omitted them:
#
#     euid=0  caps=cap_net_raw=ep
#     /etc/shadow                       r : OK
#     /etc/sudoers                      r : OK
#     /root/canary                      w : OK
#     /etc/systemd/system/probe.service w : OK
#
# euid 0 is granted by the kernel at execve whatever the bounding set says, and
# a process with euid 0 owns every root-owned file by the ordinary permission
# bits -- no capability is involved. What the omission blocked was the gid
# change, which is the legitimate path (sudo, su) and not the attack path. It
# could not deny root to anyone sudoers denies it to, only to the administrators
# sudoers allows.
#
# What keeps a session from acting on that is the sandbox below -- ProtectSystem=
# strict makes the writes above fail inside this unit -- and sudoers, which is
# where "who may become root" belongs and where an administrator will look for
# it. The rest of the bounding set still does real work: CAP_SYS_ADMIN,
# CAP_SYS_MODULE, CAP_NET_ADMIN and everything else remain out, so mounting,
# module loading and network reconfiguration stay impossible for uid 0 here.
# CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH is here for the ROOT Exec lines, not for the desktop.
# systemd stages a unit's credentials under /run/credentials/<unit>, and the
# path every credential traverses is mode 0000 root:root BY DESIGN -- systemd
# relies on DAC-bypass rather than on permissions to keep it private. A uid-0
# process whose bounding set lacks a DAC capability is judged by DAC like
# anybody else and CANNOT traverse it. Measured on 2026-09-25: with the set
# below lacking this, "ExecStartPost=!... serving-gate" found
# $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY set and the directory unreachable, fell through to an
# unauthenticated request, and Selkies answered 401 -- which failed the unit and
# tore down a desktop that had started correctly. It is not a race: watched for
# sixty seconds across thirty samples, the mode never changed.
#
# READ/SEARCH rather than CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, which was the wider spelling that
# first proved the diagnosis: this bypasses read and traverse only, never write
# permission. The blast radius is worth stating plainly rather than implying it
# is nil -- capabilities bind only uid-0 processes here, the desktop itself runs
# as the instance user and never holds them, and the set already grants
# CAP_SETUID/CAP_SETGID, so a root Exec line in this unit could already become
# any user. This adds nothing that set did not already imply.
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_RAW CAP_SETUID CAP_SETGID CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH
DevicePolicy=closed
# The GPU, where the machine has one. Both lines are inert on a machine without
# one -- the "-" makes the bind optional, and a device rule for a class of nodes
# that do not exist grants nothing -- so this is set unconditionally rather than
# made an administrator's decision to get wrong.
#
# Both are needed, and neither alone does anything. PrivateDevices= gives the
# session a private /dev holding only pseudo-devices, so the render node is not
# there to open: DeviceAllow= grants the cgroup permission and BindPaths= puts
# the node in the namespace. Without the bind the session reports "No such file
# or directory"; without the allow it reports "Permission denied" -- and the
# encoder quietly falls back to software in both cases, which looks like a slow
# machine rather than a missing device.
#
# The group that owns the node is the third piece and cannot be named here,
# because it differs between machines -- render on a stock host, plugdev inside
# these containers. "hdw4s enable" reads it off the node and writes it into a
# drop-in.
DeviceAllow=char-drm rw
BindPaths=-/dev/dri
Environment=LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1
# Not /run/user/<uid>, even though this unit now runs one desktop per account
# and could name it. That directory belongs to logind, which creates it for a
# login session and removes it with the last one; this unit has no PAM session
# and so no claim on it. RuntimeDirectory= below makes systemd the owner of
# whatever XDG_RUNTIME_DIR names, so pointing it at /run/user/<uid> would have
# stopping the desktop delete the runtime directory out from under an ssh login
# by the same account. A directory of its own costs nothing and cannot do that.
Environment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=%t/hdw4s/%i
# Put the application wrappers ahead of the real binaries. They are what
# redirects programs that ignore the XDG variables, Firefox above all.
Environment=PATH=/usr/lib/hdw4s/wrappers:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/hdw4s/hdw4s.conf
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/hdw4s/%i.conf
# PrivateTmp= gives this unit an empty /tmp, and systemd-tmpfiles only
# populates the host's, so the socket directory has to be created in
# here. ExecStartPre runs inside the namespace, so this is the private
# one and no other session can see it.
# The gated web client this session is served from, built before the session can
# start. "+" is load-bearing and is NOT a convenience: the build writes under
# /usr, which ProtectSystem=strict makes read-only for everything else in this
# unit, and it must run as root because a tree the occupant can write is a tree
# the occupant can un-gate.
#
# Rebuilt at every start rather than once when the account was enabled, because
# the tree embeds the packaged client: after an upgrade a session provisioned
# once would serve the previous release's bytes, gated but stale, with nothing
# saying so. It costs about a fifth of a second.
#
# If this fails the session does not start, which is deliberate. Selkies' own
# answer to a missing web root is one warning and the stock ungated client --
# healthy in every check this product has, with the gate silently absent.
ExecStartPre=+/usr/lib/hdw4s/hdw4s-webroot provision %i
ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/install -d -m 1777 /tmp/.X11-unix /tmp/.ICE-unix
ExecStart=/usr/lib/hdw4s/hdw4s-session %i

# What is actually SERVED, asked of the running server rather than of the file we
# generated. Selkies decides at startup whether to use the configured web root, and
# if it declines it logs one warning and serves the packaged client -- after which
# this session comes up, notifies ready, and every health check reports health while
# the gate is simply absent. Failing here stops the session, which is the point.
#
# "!" and NOT nothing: this runs as root, deliberately. A desktop may no longer open
# a connection to a session port on this machine -- that is the firewall's
# sessions_out chain, and it is what stops one visitor's desktop reaching another's
# -- and this request has exactly the shape being refused. Root is how the session's
# own check is told apart from its occupant, who cannot become root here. Run it
# without the "!" and every session on the machine fails to start.
#
# "!" and NOT "+", for the reason the ExecStartPre above sets out at length: "+"
# keeps BindPaths= and TemporaryFileSystem= while dropping ProtectHome=, which lands
# this unit's /home/user mount on the real /home.
#
# It waits for the port itself and does not rely on running after the readiness
# notification. Measured: with NotifyAccess=all something inside the desktop
# sends READY= before the streaming server has bound, so this line runs first and
# a version of it without its own wait killed every session it was meant to
# verify.
ExecStartPost=!/usr/lib/hdw4s/hdw4s-webroot serving-gate %i
KillMode=control-group
LockPersonality=yes
# Readiness is reported once the stream is accepting connections, so that
# "systemctl start" blocks until the desktop can actually be reached. The
# notification comes from a helper rather than the main process, which is why
# NotifyAccess has to be "all".
NotifyAccess=all
OOMPolicy=continue
PrivateDevices=yes
# Gives this session its own /tmp, so the X11 socket and lock file cannot
# collide with another account's desktop on this machine and there is nothing
# left to clean up when the unit stops, however it stops.
PrivateTmp=yes
ProtectClock=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
# The session IS the home directory; it cannot be hidden or made read-only.
ProtectHome=no
ProtectHostname=yes
ProtectKernelLogs=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
# Stops one session from seeing another session's processes, which matters on a
# machine that hosts desktops for several people.
ProtectProc=invisible
ProtectSystem=strict
# The session's own home is added by "hdw4s enable", which is the only
# point at which the account -- and so its home directory -- is known. A
# unit cannot name it: there is no specifier for the home of the account in
# User=, and %h is the manager's home, which here is /root.
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/hdw4s
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_NETLINK
RestrictRealtime=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
# A soak of fifty browser reconnects against one session left the streaming
# server holding 645 descriptors, from 14 at the start: roughly thirteen per
# reconnect that are never returned, mostly /proc handles and eventfds belonging
# to monitor threads it starts again each time. The default soft limit is 1024,
# so a long-lived session that has been reconnected to often enough runs out and
# starts failing in ways that look like anything but a descriptor limit.
#
# Raising the soft limit is a mitigation, not a fix; the accumulation is in the
# streaming server and is not ours to repair on this line. The hard limit was
# already 524288, so this only stops the process tripping over a default that
# was never chosen for it.
LimitNOFILE=65536

Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
RuntimeDirectory=hdw4s/%i
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0700
Slice=hdw4s-%i.slice
StateDirectory=hdw4s/%i
StateDirectoryMode=0700
TimeoutStartSec=120
Type=notify
UMask=0077
WorkingDirectory=~

# Deliberately NOT set, because each of these breaks a working desktop:
#
#   NoNewPrivileges=yes       sudo, pkexec, fusermount3 and chrome-sandbox are
#                             all setuid, and with unprivileged user namespaces
#                             restricted by default on current Ubuntu, Chrome
#                             depends on its setuid helper to start at all.
#   RestrictNamespaces=yes    breaks Chrome and Electron's sandbox, flatpak,
#                             bubblewrap and snap.
#   SystemCallFilter=...      the @system-service set excludes @mount, which
#                             gvfs and flatpak need, and @privileged, which
#                             sudo needs from a terminal in the session.
#   MemoryDenyWriteExecute=   gnome-shell is JavaScript; denying writable
#                             executable memory stops its engine from starting.
#   PrivateUsers=yes          maps every other account to "nobody", which ruins
#                             group-shared files and is worse over NFS.
#   PAMName=<stack>           would give the session a logind session, and does
#                             create one. It breaks this unit in two separate
#                             ways, both measured rather than guessed.
#
#                             pam_systemd sets XDG_RUNTIME_DIR to
#                             /run/user/<uid>, overriding the per-instance
#                             directory set above -- and RuntimeDirectory=
#                             below makes systemd the owner of whatever that
#                             names, so stopping the desktop would delete the
#                             runtime directory of any ssh login the same
#                             account has open at the time, taking that
#                             person's dbus session and gvfs mounts with it.
#
#                             And logind moves the session's processes into a
#                             session-NN.scope of its own, out of this unit's
#                             cgroup, so KillMode=control-group reaps nothing:
#                             stopping the unit left a full desktop running and
#                             the logind session stuck in "closing" forever. A
#                             short experiment accumulated twenty of them.
#
#                             None of this is needed for sudo, which works the
#                             ordinary way -- see CapabilityBoundingSet= above.

# Deliberately no [Install] section. A session is not started at boot and is
# not enabled directly: "hdw4s enable" enables hdw4s-proxy@<instance>.socket,
# and the first connection to it starts the desktop. A desktop nobody has
# connected to therefore never exists, rather than being started at boot and
# reaped later for having been idle since.
